  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Scattered showers and thunderstorms will\ncontinue into the early overnight period tonight. \n\nScattered showers and thunderstorms have developed across the \nnorthern Middle Atlantic region this afternoon as increasing \nheat and moisture have arrived along with a few upper short \nwaves. We are not expecting widespread severe weather, but with\nthe possibility of strong downpours and some damaging winds\ncould occur in a few spots, the Marginal risk for severe storms\nremains across our area for that reason.  \n\nThe storms will be slow moving, so localized flooding is possible\nthrough the tonight period as well. A slight risk for excessive\nrainfall remains for Delaware tonight. The activity is expected \nto decrease after sunset.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... A Flood Watch has been issued for Tuesday into\nWednesday as an impactful system arrives across the region. \n\nAs the upper heights continue to drop and summertime warmth and\nhumidity continue...Tuesday looks to feature the more widespread\nshowers and thunderstorms. The H5 height falls and jet dynamics\naloft will favor convective development nearly all day as \nsurface low pressure develops just to our west. This will keep a\nsteady stream of warm, moist air pumping into the area and \nkeeping the environment conditionally unstable despite cloud \ncover. PWats will rise to near 2\" by the end of the day. Some \nmodels suggest we could have a stalled or slow moving frontal \nboundary across the central or northern portion of the forecast \narea, which could act as a focusing mechanism for heavy rainfall.\nHeavy rain leading to flash flooding could end up being a bigger\nconcern and greater impact than the severe thunderstorm threat.\n\nThe latest outlooks from WPC show most of our area in a SLIGHT \nrisk for excessive rainfall, but northern NJ has a MODERATE risk\nfor excessive. A flash flood watch has been issued for Tuesday \nand into Wednesday. As for severe weather, the southern half of \nthe area including Delmarva, south NJ and metro Philadelphia \nhave a SLIGHT risk for severe thunderstorms while areas to the \nnorth are in a MARGINAL risk. Damaging winds are the primary \nthreat with thunderstorms on Tuesday. \n\nThe low pressure looks to be offshore to our east by Wednesday.\nThere is some uncertainty with regards to how fast its effects \ndiminish across the area. We'll continue with scattered showers \nand storms again by afternoon, at least across our NJ and PA \nzones, but the severe thunderstorm threat will be lower given \nlower instability. Flash flooding is possible Wednesday too.",
